\details{
An \code{mvremModel} is a list with the components \code{call}, \code{forms} (result of
\code{\link[=collectFormulas]{collectFormulas()}}), \code{data} (result of \code{\link[=prepareData]{prepareData()}}), \code{dataSummary}
(\code{\link[=summarizeData]{summarizeData()}}), \code{inla} (result of \code{\link[=buildInlaFormula]{buildInlaFormula()}}), \code{options} (how
INLA is run), \code{fit} (the INLA result, \code{NULL} if not fitted) and \code{inlaError}.
Prefer the functions below to \code{$}.

Every result (tables, plots, predictions, data summary) lists the responses
in the order in which the formulae were passed to \code{\link[=jsm]{jsm()}}: with one list
(\code{form}) the order of the list, outcomes and longitudinal responses
interleaved as given; with the pair \code{formOut} / \code{formLong} the outcomes, then
the longitudinal responses. A \code{response} argument of a result function only
selects responses; they still come in this order. \code{modelResponses()} gives it.

\code{update()} repeats the call that created the model with the given
arguments replaced (e.g. \code{update(m, family = list(y = gammaFamily()))}).
Giving \code{form} or \code{formOut} replaces all formulae: the stored formula
arguments (\code{form}, \code{formOut}, \code{formLong}) are dropped first, so
\code{update(m, form = list(...))} switches a pair-style model to one list of
formulae and \code{update(m, formOut = ..., formLong = ...)} the other way;
\code{formLong} alone replaces only the longitudinal formulae of a pair.
}
